    "body": "Freight technology vendors are selling AI agents that can benchmark rates and award freight under rules a customer sets. A new survey finds most supply chain organizations still limit AI to generating insights, and the first obstacle the research names is their own workforce.\nThe Association for Supply Chain Management (ASCM) and the Digital Supply Chain Institute (DSCI) released The People Paradox in Supply Chain AI Integration on Oct. 14. Of the supply chain professionals surveyed, 83% said their employees are unprepared to use AI effectively. The 17% who described their workforce as ready reported a 71% AI project success rate. Everyone else reported 28%, and about 64% of that group is still in the pilot or planning stage.\nAbe Eshkenazi, CEO of ASCM, said most AI sessions at supply chain conferences run through the same agenda: the challenges, the implementation, and the benefits. Every one of them ends with a reminder not to forget about the people, he said.\n“It’s interesting because from our perspective that’s where you ought to start the conversation,” Eshkenazi told FreightWaves.\nAI mostly stays in planning\nThe report scored AI adoption across 13 core supply chain functions on a scale from 1, meaning no AI use, to 5, meaning autonomous execution without human review. The average was 1.94.\nPlanning functions scored highest, led by demand planning at 2.28 and supply and inventory planning at 2.12. Execution-heavy functions trailed, and returns and reverse logistics ranked last at 1.59.\nThe report calls this keeping AI on a “short leash” and says companies doing it are using predictive tools as “little more than digital spreadsheets.” Eshkenazi reached for the same image.\n“I think we’re using it much more on the analysis and on the predictions as opposed to the doing,” he said, calling it “more of a glorified spreadsheet.”\nHe sees room for AI to take on “routine transactional decisions where AI can operate within parameters,” with people keeping the higher-risk calls on customer commitments, sourcing, and major disruptions.\n“We need to expand the AI authority, but we need to do so with confidence in the technology, and it’s not there right now,” he said.\nSkills and ROI top the barriers\nTwo barriers tied for the top spot at 33% each: a lack of workforce skills and resources, and an unclear business case or return on investment. Poor data quality followed at 25%. Executive skepticism did not make the list of main hurdles.\nEshkenazi compared AI to enterprise resource planning (ERP) systems, which were also expected to revolutionize supply chains.\n“The technology solution can’t improve bad process and it can’t improve bad data,” he said.\nCompanies should use value stream mapping and data analysis to find and fix problem areas before an ERP project starts, he said.\nHeadcount reduction is too simple a measure of AI’s return, in his view. The better one is response time: how quickly a company can recognize a disruption, weigh alternatives, and act.\n“You can’t point to a system and say it made the decision,” Eshkenazi said. “Ultimately, somebody has to own that decision for the organization.”\nInvolvement drops below the C-suite\nThe survey found that 82% of C-suite respondents lead, implement, or scale AI initiatives. At the director level, 12% lead AI efforts directly, and half of directors said they are only aware of them. The aware-only share rises to 51% of managers and 70% of staff.\nThose breakdowns rest on small samples. The survey collected 144 responses, and 107 met the criteria for analysis. Only 11 respondents were C-suite executives, and the report labels its results by job level as directional.\nFew expect AI to replace workers\nOnly 7% of respondents predict AI will directly replace workers, while 51% expect it to move people toward higher-value decision-making.\nEshkenazi said that shift carries a cost. Supply chain professionals used to build judgment by running the analysis, building the reports, and working the spreadsheets, which taught them to spot exceptions and ask whether the numbers looked right.\n“A lot of those foundational activities and experiences are now being taken over by AI, and we’re moving individuals into positions where they have to make decisions and manage without understanding the fundamentals,” he said.\nTechnology more powerful than companies are using, paired with workers who can’t critically think through what it produces, is “a very dangerous combination for organizations,” he said.\nCyber concerns outpace safeguards\nOf those surveyed, 92% expressed significant concern about cybersecurity exposure through AI-enabled suppliers and trade partners. According to the report, the average organization had adopted 2.3 of six standard cybersecurity practices.\nEshkenazi traced the exposure to the visibility push that followed the pandemic, when companies began connecting suppliers, carriers, and brokers on shared platforms.\n“It’s exposing them to significant ransomware and cybersecurity risks in their tier-three and their tier-four vendors, where predominantly they don’t have a lot of resources,” he said.\nFood distributors on a conference panel in San Antonio described one way that plays out: a compromised carrier email that led to a fraudulent pickup of a cold-storage load .\nCalling a tier-four vendor for inventory and delivery status isn’t an option, he said. “Huge opportunity but also significant risk,” he said.\nStart with one process\nEshkenazi’s first recommendation is to pick one high-value process, such as a key customer process, supplier, or route, and involve the people who run it.\n“Then establish measurable outcomes,” he said. “First, you have to identify where you’re starting from.”\nThat work has to reach below the leadership level, he said, so employees see AI as a path to more valuable work.\n“Just because it’s a technology solution doesn’t make it autonomous and doesn’t make it better if you don’t have qualified processes, if you don’t have knowledgeable workers, and if you don’t have clean data,” Eshkenazi said.\nWhy it matters: Vendors are selling AI that can act on freight decisions, but most supply chain organizations still use it for analysis. The ASCM and DSCI survey ties that caution to unready workers, unclear returns, and thin cybersecurity safeguards.\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper o